---
import Layout from 'src/layouts/Layout.astro';
import GitHub from 'src/components/GitHub.astro';

import ImageGrid from 'src/components/ImageGrid.astro';
---

<Layout page="Year 3">
	<div class="project">
		<h3>Games Preproduction/IP3: Puffy MacPuffer</h3>
		<GitHub author="dylanusinggithub" repo="Puffy-McPuffer" extra="https://kokwithoutak.github.io"></GitHub>

		<a href="/Year3/PuffyTitle.gif"><img src="/Year3/PuffyTitle.gif" style="float: left;" alt="/Year3/AltTitle/PuffyTitle.txt"/></a>

		<p>
			The real hightlight of this year. <a href="https://www.scottishopera.org.uk/pop-up-opera-2025/">Puffy MacPuffer</a> is an opera about the titular 
			character Puffy a puffer boat and their adventure through the five Crabbit Canals. We initally started with only a team of six: three programmers, 
			one artist and two designers, though one had to unfortunetly leave after the first week. We met with out client, Ian Piercy, once every few weeks
			for brain storming.
		</p>

		<p>
			The first half of the year was spent in preproduction, the first few weeks was comming up with ideas and looking at the <a href="https://gcugames.itch.io/puffy-mcpuffers-grand-canal-adventure">
			previous year's attempt</a>, we came up with with 3 ideas:
		</p>

		<div id="PuffyPitch">
			<ImageGrid location="Year3" images={[`IP3Concept1.png`, `IP3Concept2.png`, `IP3Concept3.png`]} link="PitchSlides.pdf"></ImageGrid>
		</div>

		<a href="/Year3/IP3GyroTest.png"><img src="/Year3/IP3GyroTest.png" alt=["/Year3/AltText/IP3GyroTest.txt"] style="float: right; height:300px; width:150px"/></a>
		<p>
			Concept 1 was a point and click 2D scroller where you interact with the canals and they would give you objectives that would allow you to progress with some extra
			goodies to ogle at when clicking on them.

			Concept 2 was a variation of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pipe_Mania">Pipe Mania</a> where you're placing down sections of canals to guide Puffy around,
			and they would win by going through X amount of pieces. Sometimes the board would have pre-placed titles to help or hinder the player, some levels would have a 
			special mode making the player need to go through all the in addition to the usual requirement. And an alterntive mode for the board to be completely filled and
			a path is created by rotating the pieces.

			Concept 3 was a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pok%C3%A9mon_Snap">Pokémon Snap</a>-esque game where you're on the clyde canal and are taking photos of the
			landmarks, whenever you did this infomation would appear about that landmark, you would be scored on how well it was taken and if the object was intresting. All the photos
			taken could then be viewed in a gallery. This game was to be more of a learning experiance similar to the original show itself.
		</p>

		<p>
			For the Pokémon Snap idea I was so excited for it I went ahead and try and make a demo of the gyro controls we wanted. This was done by building an android app to then test,
			though the idea was this was to end up being played on iPads due to - at the time - the increased usage of them in educational spaces. Sadly due to issues with <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gimbal_lock">
			gimbal lock</a>, drift and the more fundamental issue of it not working on WebGL (our target platform).
		</p>	

		<p>
			Concept 2 and 3 were our favourties, especially concept 3 though we were apprehensive about concept 3's due to only having the one artist and only having a few months
			to create it all, and the Lectures and client agreed so we went with concept 2 but were told to expand it.
		</p>
		<p>
			After that meeting we planned to see Ian at his studio for a brainstorming session the following day, where we ended up landing on the idea of a minigame collection; we'd
			keep Pipe Mania and make it about 10 seconds long per level and add harder variations of that and another minigame I came up with based upon the hectic waters that form whilst 
			going up a lock.
		</p>

		<p>
			How we worked initally in preproduction was each developer was in charge of a minigame: I had Lock Balance, Dylan had Pipe Mania and Jack had Canal Crusier, though we did of course
			still help each other whenever needed either during our weekly meetings in class or the odd message on Discord, like when I converted the old movement in Canal Crusier to no longer
			be lane based and work with unity's control system for added support.
		</p>

		<ImageGrid images={[`PuffyLockBalance1.gif`, `PuffyLockBalance2.gif`, `PuffyLockBalance3.gif`]} location="Year3"></ImageGrid>

		<p>
			I started work on the first minigame Lock Balance, or at the time called "Canal Balance", I made the boat move using one dimensional perlin noise, one for X, Y and rotation, for 
			some smooth randomness that was easily controllable. Originally it was a top down view hence the equal vertical movement but due to perception issues during a demonstration this 
			was quickly changed to a side-on view.
		</p>

		<p>
			This was quickly followed up in the next 2 weeks with an indicator for the water's direction (which weirdly got turned into being wind instead?), a stun system for losing control breifly once hit, a short 
			"Mario Party-esque intro cutscene" of puffy entering the lock and centring himself, the ability to actually lose by crashing into the sides and incredibly important a falling objects 
			system for Puffy to collect and dodge to fit in with  the new win condiditon which was score based instead of the old system of having to survive X amount of seconds. 
		</p>

		<p>
			Inbetween doing that I created a website to allow us to quickly test builds using github pages with a autodeployer as the WebGL builds require server to be ran on with the added bonus of 
			being able to showcase the progress to the client and lecutres easier. All of which was such an useful tool that I'll be 100% using some similar process for any future projects going forward.
		</p>

		<p>
			At this point we were at week 10 of 12 and we had the final vertical slice presention due so I ended picking up some slack from the Canal Cruiser minigame due to it lagging behind slightly.
			I started with one of the feedback we got from the lecturers to change its movement from lane-based to more be free, 
		</p>

	</div>  
</Layout>
